# DME DocLock Product Discovery Document

**Product:** DME DocLock — “Proof before ship.”  
**Decision date:** September 22, 2026  
**Discovery team:** Product discovery lead; user researcher; DME/Medicare domain expert; approval gate  
**Decision:** **GO to a tightly bounded, paid-design-partner v1, subject to the conditions in §8.**

> **Discovery status.** This is a decision-quality discovery plan and evidence synthesis, not a claim that primary research has already occurred. Public evidence is cited inline. Persona economics, journey times, and supplier-level rates are explicit hypotheses to validate with observed workflow data. “Improper payment” is not synonymous with fraud, denial, or recoverable supplier revenue.

## Executive summary

DME DocLock should be built as a **pre-fulfillment Medicare documentation control plane**, not another order-entry, billing, or clinical-decision system. Its job is to select the right approved rule version for HCPCS, date of service (DOS), beneficiary jurisdiction, and workflow; map source evidence to those criteria; identify administrative gaps; coordinate a cure; and require an attributable human release, hold, or override. The durable artifact is a sealed provenance packet.

The wedge is compelling. CMS reported FY2024 DMEPOS payments of about $9.0B, projected improper payments of $1.9B, and a 21.4% improper-payment rate ([CMS FY2024 supplemental data](https://www.cms.gov/files/document/2024-medicare-fee-service-supplemental-improper-payment-data.pdf)). For glucose monitors, CMS reports $278.5M projected improper payments at 25.2%; 67.6% was attributed to no documentation and 26.6% to insufficient documentation ([CMS glucose-monitor compliance tips](https://www.cms.gov/training-education/medicare-learning-networkr-mln/compliance/medicare-provider-compliance-tips/glucose-monitoring-supplies)). For lumbar-sacral orthoses, the rate was 54.4%, with 64.4% attributed to insufficient documentation ([CMS spinal-orthoses compliance tips](https://www.cms.gov/training-education/medicare-learning-networkr-mln/compliance/medicare-provider-compliance-tips/spinal-orthoses)). These are program-level estimates, not a supplier’s avoidable loss; they establish exposure and documentation salience, not DocLock’s ROI.

Operational urgency increased in 2026. Seven HCPCS codes joined the Required Prior Authorization List effective April 13, taking the operational list from 67 to 74; eight more become effective October 28, taking it to 82. CMS also begins probationary prior authorization on October 15, 2026 for certain items furnished by newly enrolled DMEPOS suppliers and suppliers undergoing certain changes of ownership ([CMS required PA initiative](https://www.cms.gov/data-research/monitoring-programs/medicare-fee-service-compliance-programs/prior-authorization-and-pre-claim-review-initiatives/prior-authorization-process-certain-durable-medical-equipment-prosthetics-orthotics-and-supplies); [CMS probationary PA](https://www.cms.gov/data-research/monitoring-programs/medicare-fee-service-compliance-programs/prior-authorization-pre-claim-review-initiatives/probationary-prior-authorization-process-newly-enrolled-suppliers-certain-durable-medical-equipment)). The product must therefore treat policy effective dating as a safety-critical function.

The recommendation is conditional because three uncertainties dominate: whether enough defects are curable before shipment, whether “release-ready” precision can safely reach 95% with zero silent policy-version errors, and whether clinic guests will complete requests without adopting software. The v1 gate is therefore the approved brief’s three validation highlights: **8/12 qualified suppliers evidence the problem; 1,000-order retrospective benchmark clears quality and review-time thresholds; and three partners pay at least $24K ACV and produce measurable 90-day workflow impact.**

