## 6. Value proposition and pricing hypotheses

### 6.1 Value proposition

**For Medicare-heavy DME operators who lose time and leverage chasing fragmented evidence, DME DocLock creates a source-linked, effective-dated proof gate before shipment. Unlike an ERP, e-prescribing network, or generic document AI, it overlays existing systems, lets clinics cure a precise task without buying software, and preserves a human-attributable packet—without making medical-necessity decisions.**

Value pillars: (1) prevent curable defects while leverage remains; (2) reduce intake touches and elapsed chase time; (3) make release decisions consistent and visible; (4) accelerate audit response; (5) prove which policy and source supported the action.

### 6.2 ROI model—not a promise

For a 3,000-order/month location, use conservative, locally measurable inputs:

- Orders/year: 36,000.
- Chase incidence: 18% (discovery hypothesis).
- Current extra touches: 2.5 × 7 minutes.
- Reduction target: 30%.
- Loaded intake labor: $30/hour.
- Documentation-denial incidence: 3% of orders; average allowed $250; avoid 30%.
- Denial rework: $25/claim (vendor benchmark, validate locally).
- Audit packets: 240/year; save 1.25 hours at $50/hour.

**Annual benefit hypothesis**

1. Intake labor: 36,000 × 18% × 2.5 × 7/60 × $30 × 30% = **$17,010**.
2. Avoided denial rework: 36,000 × 3% × 30% × $25 = **$8,100**.
3. Allowed dollars no longer exposed/delayed: 36,000 × 3% × 30% × $250 = **$81,000**. This is **not booked as benefit** until the supplier proves incremental collection or cash-timing value.
4. Audit preparation: 240 × 1.25 × $50 = **$15,000**.

Hard labor benefit is **$40,110/year** ($17,010 + $8,100 + $15,000). At $24,000 annual subscription, gross hard-benefit ROI is **1.67×** and simple payback **7.2 months**, excluding implementation and any verified collections. With a $20,000 first-year implementation fee, first-year hard benefit does not fully clear total fees; enterprise/multi-location economics require shared implementation and measured denial benefit. This honest result makes the paid pilots essential.

CMS’s $1.9B/21.4%, CGM $278.5M/25.2%, and orthosis rates should size risk and prioritize categories—not be multiplied by one supplier’s revenue as “savings.” Improper payments can be overpayments or underpayments and include non-documentation causes.

### 6.3 Pricing experiments

| Model | Hypothesis | Advantages | Risks | Experiment / pass |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Per active location | **$24K/location/year** + **$15K–$25K** implementation; volume/family bands | Budgetable, aligned to approved brief, supports control-plane value | Penalizes distributed footprints; “active location” ambiguity; weak volume alignment | Present to 12 buyers; ≥3 paid at ≥$24K ACV-equivalent; ≥70% understand metric without negotiation |
| Per order | $2–$5 per Medicare order, monthly minimum | Scales with use; easy unit economics | Discourages running every order; disputes over resupply/order; variable budget; could feel like transaction tax | Van Westendorp/Gabor-Granger plus invoice simulation; reject if >20% intend selective use |
| Tiered subscription **(recommended test)** | Core: $24K/location/year incl. volume/category allowance; Growth/Enterprise add SSO, API, more families, analytics, governance; overage only at high bands | Predictability plus cost-to-serve coverage; enterprise expansion | Packaging complexity; category gating can confuse | Three proposal A/B sequences; pass if ≥50% choose a paid tier without bespoke core discount |

No contingency fees: they encourage overclaiming and confuse documentation controls with recovery. Implementation covers mapping, security, criteria activation, workflow configuration, training, and baseline measurement—not custom clinical policy creation.

